title: Under one's own note, deleting the fork drawn in the original's place still takes the original out of the note too, as before. A question for Jeff.
pid: 963e102f-92ce-4593-84e1-ddd013745273
body: |2-
  In Jeff's mailbox that is his fork of Top's 'to Jeff', where a reply
  files the message and his fork of the message is drawn in its place:
  deleting that fork there both takes the reply back and dismisses the
  message (the note subscribes, so the message is hidden), as it did
  before. To keep the message filed, he would delete the fork from a
  view of the fork itself. The other way, built first and taken out:
  one rule everywhere, the original kept in whatever list the fork
  stood in, which is simpler and agrees with how validation already
  counts such a child; but it changes what that gesture did in his own
  notes, which the mission said not to do without asking. It needs the
  user's ownership of the parent in the traversal, now passed in as
  'IsOwned'; dropping the 'of_another' test in 'content_members' would
  give the other way.
